% This is a fictional demo paper shipped with ploma. Its system, numbers and results are invented.
% It exists so the README film can be reproduced: ploma runs on a copy of this directory.
% The references are real and verified; nothing else in the paper describes real work.
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage{booktabs}

\title{Tracing Agentic Workloads at Scale}
\author{ploma demo}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
LLM agents issue long chains of tool calls, and current tracing stacks record each call without the decision that caused it.
We present Hawser, a tracer that records the causal link from each model decision to the tool results it consumed.
Across 1200 tasks on three agent frameworks, Hawser cuts median trace-query latency by 27.4\% against an OpenTelemetry pipeline.
It stores 1.1 times the bytes of that pipeline.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/intro}
\input{sections/method}
\input{sections/results}

\bibliographystyle{plain}
\bibliography{refs}

\end{document}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We ran 1200 tasks on three agent frameworks and issued the same set of causal queries against each tracing back end.
Table~\ref{tab:results} reports the reduction in median trace-query latency that Hawser achieves against two baselines, and its storage relative to the OpenTelemetry pipeline.
Against an OpenTelemetry pipeline, Hawser cuts median latency by 27.4\%.
Against raw logs searched by identifier, it cuts median latency by 41.2\%.
Hawser stores 1.1 times the bytes of the OpenTelemetry pipeline, because it keeps the causal edges as well as the events.

